      "conclusion_deduplication": {
        "body_markdown": "US consumer inflation turned higher in July after declining in June, although the annual rate moved below its prior reading.\n\nThe Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers rose 0.1 percent on a seasonally adjusted basis after falling 0.4 percent in June. Over the 12 months ending in July, the all-items index increased 3.4 percent, following a 3.5 percent rise for the period ending in June. [www.bls.gov](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/cpi.pdf)\n\n## Shelter leads the monthly increase\n\nShelter rose 0.1 percent and accounted for roughly two-thirds of the monthly all-items increase. Food also advanced 0.1 percent, with food away from home up 0.3 percent. Energy moved in the opposite direction, declining 1.5 percent as gasoline fell 2.9 percent. [www.bls.gov](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/cpi.pdf)\n\nCore CPI, which excludes food and energy, increased 0.2 percent after being unchanged in June. Its 12-month increase was 2.5 percent, following a 2.6 percent rise for the period ending in June. [www.bls.gov](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/cpi.pdf)\n\nThe component mix leaves a clear distinction between the headline number and its drivers: shelter supplied most of the monthly increase, while falling energy costs restrained it. On an annual basis, energy was still up 14.7 percent and food was up 3.0 percent. [www.bls.gov](https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/cpi.pdf)",
